findings.body:
The move deleted `useRouter` from the import and removed `const router = useRouter();`. The component still uses `router` in five places:

::code{file="apps/web/modules/bookings/components/BookerWebWrapper.tsx" lines="183-196"}

The `[router]` dependency array passed to `useCallback` runs on every render. That throws `ReferenceError: router is not defined` as soon as `BookerWebWrapperComponent` mounts. Every page using it breaks: user booking pages, team booking pages, the cached team view, instant meetings and `/d/[link]`. The `router.push` calls on lines 228, 232 and 238 are also unresolved, so TypeScript fails too.

Put the hook back:

::suggestion{file="apps/web/modules/bookings/components/BookerWebWrapper.tsx" lines="4-5"}
import { useSearchParams, usePathname, useRouter } from "next/navigation";
import { useMemo, useCallback, useEffect } from "react";

Then add `const router = useRouter();` back at the top of `BookerWebWrapperComponent`.

The original function in `HostEditDialogs.tsx` returned `priorityB - priorityA` when weights were equal (higher priority first). The relocated copy returns `priorityA - priorityB`:

::diff{file="packages/lib/bookings/hostGroupUtils.ts" lines="41-60"}

With RR weights enabled, hosts with equal weight now sort lowest priority first. The non-weighted branch still sorts highest first, so the two branches disagree. This affects host order in `useEventTypeForm`'s default values, `EventTeamAssignmentTab` and the weight/priority dialogs. This was supposed to be a pure move.

::suggestion{file="packages/lib/bookings/hostGroupUtils.ts" lines="52-53"}
    if (weightA === weightB) {
      return priorityB - priorityA;

There were no unit tests for `sortHosts` before or after the move. It's a pure function, so it's easy to cover and that would have caught this.
